      "explanation": "The exponents differ by one, so \\(4^x=4\\cdot4^{x-1}\\). This lets us factor the subtraction:\n\\[\n4^x-4^{x-1}\n=(4-1)4^{x-1}=24.\n\\]\nThus \\(4^{x-1}=8\\). Writing both sides as powers of \\(2\\) gives\n\\[\n2^{2x-2}=2^3.\n\\]\nPowers of \\(2\\) increase strictly with their exponents, so their equality implies\n\\[\n2x-2=3,\n\\qquad x=\\frac52.\n\\]\nNow \\(2x=5>0\\), and the fractional exponent gives\n\\[\n(2x)^x=5^{5/2}=5^2\\sqrt{5}=25\\sqrt{5}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice C.",
      "id": "factor-and-compare-powers",
      "sourceRef": "amc/1958_AMC_12_Q20#factor-and-compare-powers",
      "title": "Factor the common power"
